    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nHyundai Recalls Kona Electric and Veloster N for Airbag Software Concern\n24 photos\nPhoto: Hyundai\nBelieve it or not, Hyundai spent more than two years investigating a problem that can leave two very different vehicles without functioning airbags. The root cause behind this condition? According to Hyundai, corrupted data inside the airbag control unit. The subsequent recall covers 37,901 examples of the Kona Electric and the Veloster N, and that's only in the US market.\nIn this part of the world, no fewer than 331 unique reports alleging the condition have been received by the South Korean manufacturer since October 2019. None involved an injury, and that sure is fortunate considering the failure can disable the seatbelt pretensioners as well.\nThe recall population splits between 29,058 examples of the 2019 through 2023 model year Kona Electric and 8,843 units of the 2019 through 2022 model year Veloster N. And yes, I'm missing the quirky Veloster N as much as the next guy. The common denominator here is the airbag control software. More to the point, a reboot at the wrong moment disables the airbags.\nThe subject control unit monitors crash signals, commanding the deployment of the airbags and seatbelt pretensioners. Obviously enough, a pretensioner is designed to tighten the belt during a collision, keeping the occupant in the right position before the airbags do their job.\nHyundai determined that airbag control unit data can become corrupted while said unit writes information to its internal memory. A reboot or interruption in electrical power during the writing operation leaves the controller with corrupted data, compromising the system.\nPhoto: Hyundai Motor Company The compromised state becomes evident once the airbag malfunction indicator illuminates. Hyundai filed this recall with the NHTSA as campaign 26V728, but the automaker calls it 311. Sadly, I cannot guarantee that anyone in charge of safety at Hyundai Motor America loves 311 the band.\nAnyway, the ACU responds by deactivating the airbag system, leaving the occupants without the expected restraint protection in the event of a crash that calls for the deployment of the airbags and pretensioners. ZF Group is listed in the recall documentation as the supplier of the ACU, which features no fewer than three distinct part numbers in Hyundai's parts system.\nHMA's North America Safety Office started looking into this matter on August 19, 2024, after identifying an increasing number of warranty claims involving the airbag warning light. Five ACUs were recovered that year for examination. Hyundai and ZF worked together, but finding a reproducible fault took an excruciatingly long time.\nElectrical tests were conducted between May 2025 and August 2025, followed by vehicle-level replication tests between September 2025 and August 2026. Ultimately, the mad lads did it, confirming the root cause and becoming aware of the safety risks posed by this issue.\nDealers are to update all suspect vehicles, albeit only with the specialized service tool via the OBD-II port. There is no over-the-air option available. Customers will be formally notified in January 2027. In the meantime, the NHTSA's VIN lookup system can already recognize the affected vehicles.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHyundai Kona Hyundai Veloster Hyundai recall software\nDownload: Hyundai Veloster N and Kona Electric airbag control unit software recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1968 Shelby GT500 Ragtop 4-Speed Sat 26 Years Untouched, Car Nut Finds It in Pieces, It's a 1-of-9",
